## 3. What 832 MHz ARMv6 / 384 MB actually budgets

Estimates, to be replaced by measurement in U2.0:

| Quantity | Estimate | Basis |
|---|---|---|
| Effective integer throughput | 250–400 MIPS | ARM11 single-issue-ish, L1-resident, soft float |
| Cached memory bandwidth | ~150–250 MB/s | single ARM11 core, word-at-a-time, no NEON |
| Full-screen fill, 32 bpp | ~0.4–0.8 ms | 76,800 px × ~4–8 cyc |
| 120×160 logical fill, RGB565 | ~0.03–0.06 ms | 19,200 px — **16× cheaper** |
| Dirty-row flush of 40 rows (32 bpp) | ~38 KB moved | ~0.2–0.4 ms if cached |
| Bitmap text, 40×25 chars @8×8 | ~0.05 ms | ~16 stores/char |
| LVGL RAM (heap + draw buffer + static) | 100–160 KB | `LV_MEM_SIZE` 64 KB + 38 KB partial buf |
| LVGL binary, static musl, pruned | 200–400 KB | `-Os`, gc-sections, no animations |
| Free RAM for the whole session | ~40–90 MB | 290 MB user RAM minus live Android |

**The dominant unknown is whether `mmap` of `fb0` is cached or uncached on this driver.** If the mapping is uncached/write-through, per-pixel stores go straight at the bus and every pixel operation gets 3–10× more expensive. Your own note says the lower driver uses a "DMA/write-combine framebuffer" — that is a red flag. **This single fact determines color depth, buffer strategy, render resolution and flush cadence. Measure it before writing any UI code.**

