### LVGL 8.3 — why this, concretely

**In favour, with evidence:**

- **Zero external dependencies** for the fbdev + evdev path. v8's indev driver is a self-contained `read()` loop over `struct input_event`. Note the v9 docs say explicitly *"The driver always requires `libevdev`"* — that dependency, plus v9's `lv_conf.h`/fontconfig changes, is exactly why **v8.3 is the right pin, not v9**.
- **v8.3 is the long-support branch.** LVGL's own release table: v8.3 (6 Jul 2022) → supported to 1 Jan 2025, and every minor from v8 gets a year of support.
- **The display model fits your hardware exactly.** v8 uses `lv_disp_draw_buf_t` + a `flush_cb`, in `LV_DISP_BUF_MODE_PARTIAL`. You write a ~40-line `flush_cb` that: expands RGB565 → XRGB8888 into the back page of the 240×640 virtual fb, then `ioctl(0x46ff, {top,bottom})`. That is the whole integration. No X, no compositor, no mode setting.
- **Partial rendering is the default**, so you only push dirty regions — which the vendor ioctl is literally designed for.
- **`LV_COLOR_DEPTH 16` is available in v8.** In v9, `lv_color_t` is *always RGB888 regardless of `LV_COLOR_DEPTH`* (v9 changelog, migration guide) — that alone breaks the RGB565-internal plan. Another decisive reason for v8.
- **MIT licence.** No copyleft entanglement in a project you intend to keep yours.
- **Plain-Makefile integration.** LVGL ships `src/lv.mk` for use from an existing Makefile — no CMake, no Meson, no `pkg-config` in your static-musl toolchain. *Verify this file exists in the pinned v8.3 tree.*
- **Custom themes are the first-class feature.** Retro look is a theme file, not a rewrite.

**Against, honestly:**

- It is an **MCU toolkit**, so it has no window manager, no app model, no desktop — you get widgets, nothing else. Everything that makes it a "Pocket-PC" is yours to build. That is not a flaw; it is the reason to pick it.
- Its default visual language (rounded, antialiased, shadowed, animated) is the *opposite* of the target aesthetic. You will disable shadow/gradient/AA/animations and write a custom theme. Budget a day for this.
- Widget bloat if you don't prune. `lv_conf.h` discipline is mandatory.
- v8.3 is a maintenance branch, not the tip. Accept the freeze; it's an asset for a 5-year hobby project.

### SDL 1.2.15 — the underrated accelerant

Don't dismiss this; it's the reason you'd get a *retro* feel for free, and it's proven in exactly your configuration:

- `bkauler/gui_engine`: a framebuffer UI example **statically linked with musl + SDL 1.2 = 306 KB**; with SDL_ttf + freetype + png = 560 KB.
- `uablrek/sdl-without-x11`: builds SDL 1.2 from scratch with **musl, no X**, and gets **ScummVM building and starting**.
- Configure for framebuffer only with `--enable-video-fbcon --without-x` and disable everything else.

**But** SDL 1.2 is a *video/sound/events* library, not a widget toolkit — you'd write every widget yourself anyway, and it manages the whole screen itself, which fights your partial/dirty-row model. Its fbcon path has no touchscreen support without tslib or custom event injection.

**Verdict: keep it as a compatibility layer for timeboxed app experiments (ScummVM, SDL games, SDL_mixer audio), never as the shell.**

### DirectFB / DirectFB2

Genuinely the right *shape* (real window system, direct fbdev, no X). But: last official DirectFB release **1.7.7 in 2015** (FOSDEM 2022 slides, Denis Kropp); DirectFB2 revived 2021–22, ~1 MB library, armv7-tested, young; **LGPL-2.1** is awkward for a statically-linked self-contained payload; autotools build; and its Fusion/surface/blitter machinery is designed for multi-process app environments you don't have. For a single-process retro shell, it buys you a window system you won't use and costs you licence and build pain. Keep on the shelf.

### X11 + matchbox — the one to definitively rule out

This is the postmarketOS/maemo/Openmoko path and it is the most likely wrong answer. On 64-bit x86, the Matchbox session costs **matchbox-window-manager 11 MB RSS, matchbox-panel 14 MB, mb-applet-menu-launcher 11 MB, matchbox-desktop 18 MB** (gilesorr.com WM memory survey) — *before* Xorg, *before* any GTK app, and *without* ARMv6's missing NEON hurting pixman. Add glibc (musl + modern Xorg is a known headache), fontconfig/freetype, ~40 shared libraries. You have maybe 40–90 MB of *total* free RAM while Android is resident. Ruled out on arithmetic, not taste. (The classic counter-example — the maemo list post showing GTK+/X fitting in <32 MB on a 200 MHz ARM with 64 MB RAM — is precisely a budget you don't have.)

The *ideas* are still worth stealing: **Matchbox shows one window at a time**. That's your view-stack model.

### Qt

Qt 4 QWS with `-qt-gfx-linuxfb -qt-kbd-linuxinput -qt-mouse-linuxinput` is architecturally the correct model and is exactly what I mean by "imitate this, don't install this". But Qt 4.8 is EOL, C++, and multi-megabyte; **Qt 5.0 removed QWS entirely** (Qt 5.10 embedded-linux docs), leaving only the `linuxfb` QPA plugin, and Qt 5/6 mkspecs assume ARMv7+ glibc. Reject.

### GPU

The VideoCore IV is real (1 Gpixel fill advertised) but on 2.6.35 there's no DRM/KMS, no EGL, no open driver — only Broadcom blobs tied to the Android HAL. Unreachable from the Linux payload. Reject, and stop thinking about it.

