## 9. Unknowns to resolve on-device before committing

| Unknown | How to resolve | If it goes wrong |
|---|---|---|
| Is `fb0` mmap cached or uncached? | `totoro-fbbench`, and compare `/proc/self/smaps` `VmFlags` | Render to heap + `write()` rows instead of mmap |
| Does the CPU have VFP / DSP / Thumb-2? | `Features:` line in `/proc/cpuinfo` | Keep `-march=armv6 -mfloat-abi=soft` unconditionally |
| Actual `init.rc` service names + `onrestart` graph | read `/system/etc/init/*.rc` on device | Android returns after ~2 min (the documented linuxdeploy failure) |
| Other private ioctls (flip, dirty-rect, backlight, TE) | read `lcdfb.c` ioctl table; probe harmlessly | Stay with row-range flush; bracket session by backlight sysfs |
| `lv.mk` present in the pinned v8.3 tree | check the tree | Amalgamate LVGL sources manually (trivial) |
| Real frame costs | `totoro-fbbench` + an LVGL benchmark mode | Drop render scale to 80×120, or to BASIC backend |

One toolchain warning worth stating plainly: **build everything with the same flags that produced your working `totoro-musl-test` (`arm-linux-musleabi`, `-march=armv6`, soft float).** Autotools-based dependencies — SDL above all — habitually default to `-march=armv7-a` and hard-float and will SIGILL on this SoC. That is the single most common way this kind of project quietly wastes a week.

---

## Summary

The conventional answers don't fit. X11 + matchbox is unaffordable by an order of magnitude; Qt Embedded is the right *model* but the wrong dependency tree; DirectFB is architecturally apt but LGPL'd, autotools-heavy, and unmaintained in its 1.x line; SDL 1.2 is a proven musl+framebuffer accelerator worth keeping in your pocket, but it is not a widget toolkit. **LVGL 8.3 — pinned, not v9 — is the only option that gives a full widget layer with zero external dependencies, a partial-rendering model that matches your dirty-row ioctl exactly, a native RGB565 internal format the panel actually is, MIT licensing, and a plain-Makefile build that drops into your existing static-musl toolchain.** Everything above it — the session manager, the view-stack shell, the app ABI, the retro design language — should be yours and toolkit-independent, with a ~1.5 kLOC hand-rolled backend as insurance. And the two things to do before writing any UI code are: **measure the framebuffer, and prove you can hold the display for five minutes and give it back.**



